<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aratus of Sicyon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">271–213 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Aratos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aratus of Sicyon was a Greek statesman who freed his city from its tyrant in 251 BCE, brought it into the Achaean League and led the league for over thirty years as its general. He seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acrocorinth">Acrocorinth</a> from its Macedonian garrison in 243 BCE and expanded the league across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, but when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> defeated him he called in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Antigonus III Doson</a>, reversing his anti-Macedonian policy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon.jpg" alt="Aratus of Sicyon" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by Guillaume Rouille (1518?-1589) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aratus_of_Sicyon.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The decisive expansion beyond Achaea began in 251 BCE, when the young <b>Aratus</b> overthrew the tyrant Nicocles of Sicyon and brought his city, which was not Achaean, into the league.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Through Polybius, Livy and Plutarch's lives of Demetrius, <b>Aratus</b> and Aemilius Paullus, the dynasty became for later readers a classic example of Hellenistic kingship and of its defeat by Rome.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From 251 BCE, when <b>Aratus of Sicyon</b> freed his city and joined it to the Achaean League, the league drove them out or persuaded them to step down: Lydiadas of Megalopolis laid down his tyranny in 235 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Macedonian kings garrisoned Acrocorinth until <b>Aratus</b> took it for the Achaean League in 243 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/corinth">Corinth</a></li><li><blockquote>In 241 BCE the Aetolian League sacked it, and <b>Aratus</b> routed the Aetolians in its streets.</blockquote><a href="/state/pellene">Pellene</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Aratus</b> freed Sicyon from tyranny in 251 BCE and joined it to the Achaean League, which he then led.</blockquote><a href="/state/sicyon">Sicyon</a></li></ul></section>
